Prague – Criminals in the current corruption case allegedly worked, saying the accused former vice-chairman of the Football Association of the Czech Republic Roman Berbr was supposed to have promised a million crowns from Prague Slavia for the league title in the 2018/19 season.
Efotbal.cz reported that it had accessed a part of the police files.
Jaroslav Tvrdík, Chairman of the Board of Directors of Vršovice Club, said that the red-and-whites did not commit any corruption.
The server published a transcript of police wiretaps, including its former sports director Jan Nezmar, who ended up in the championship club last summer.
According to the file, the former influential red-and-while official was in frequent contact with both Berbre and the former sports director of the then second-league Vyšehrad, Roman Rogoz, who is also among the accused in the case.
Criminals reportedly worked with information that Slavia promised Berbra a financial reward when he won the title.
2019 the SK Slavia Praha team won the title in the first league.
The police authority had the knowledge that Roman (Berbr) should have a million-dollar bribe from the officials of SK Slavia Praha for winning in the league, the server quoted the file as saying.
A day later, according to the criminals, Berbr met not only with Nezmar, but also with the Chairman of the Board of Slavie Tvrdík.
According to the server, the file no longer indicates whether the police are still dealing with this information.
Harder rejected any corruption.
Between 2015 and 2017 we actively tried to change the circumstances in Czech football and offered an opposing alternative to its development.
We have never committed illegal acts, have not sought to influence referees in violation of fair play rules, and we have not provided anyone with any financial performance in this context," Tvrdík told Seznam News.
Among other things, Nezmar indiscriminately insults some former Slavia dark-skinned players and also slanders his former boss Tvrdík.
In mid-October, in mid-October, a police crackdown in several places, including the FAČR’s Prague headquarters, sparked an alleged influencing matches through referees.
The highest-ranking in the affair is Berbr, who no longer figures in any of the football functions.
In mid-January, like the former sports director of Vyšehrad, Rogoz was released from custody.
