The first swallows
The Covid pandemic is slowing, however, experts for the coming weeks are not expecting any major reversal.
According to statistical models, the onslaught in hospitals will last for some time and a new unknown has been added to the pandemic equation: the omicron variant, which is very likely to spread faster than the predominant Delta.
At the same time, it is not yet possible to say with absolute certainty whether it can cause a more serious course, to the extent that vaccination or post-infective immunity acquired by the previous illness helps against it.
But an unexpected phenomenon on the plus side has also entered the Covid equation this week: the possibility of treatment.
A new drug has arrived in the Czechnia, an antiviral drug molnupiravir, which reduces the risk of severe course of the disease and the associated hospitalization by a third and can be treated at home.
It should soon be supplemented by paxlovid from Pfizer, which reports a success rate of 85 percent of the results so far.
However, in addition to hoping to expand the portfolio of tools useful in the fight against the coronavirus pandemic, the first deliveries of molnupiravir to the Czech Republic have also highlighted the question of how prepared the local administration is for incoming medicines.
As has been said, Merck's molnupiravir will reach domestic patients first.
The company was the first to end up on the tape because the drug began to develop long before the outbreak of the current pandemic in order to find a suitable treatment for horse viral disease on the South American continent.
