Let's rewrite history, seriously
Emmanuel Macron presented the priorities of France's EU presidency before the weekend - beginning in January - and it was spectacular.
Macron spoke for over an hour, during which he unveiled the Presidency’s logo, called for the protection of Europeans – at work, on the streets – and mentioned so many actions that they could not be done in six months.
However, French politicians love it that way, and voters like it too.
Macron's supporters between the right and the left agree little, but they do on Europe.
There will be new elections of the head of state in France in April.
The electoral calendar also affected the priorities themselves.
Among other things, the French leader mentioned that historians should write "one history of Europe" and France is ready to create conditions for such work by historians.
A number of commentators immediately rushed with criticism that Macron is interested in pro-European propaganda and rewrites history.
In fact, he's trying to prevent the rewriting of history.
The far-right candidate for French President Éric Zemmour is going around France right now with the proposition that the regime from Vicha, who collaborated with Hitler during the Second World War, was not so bad, and is quite successful with the French.
Let's take Macron's idea for a history book seriously, and let's not look at what's going on in France.
Wouldn't it be needed?
Students in European countries learn history as the story of us versus them and never as the story of the whole.
Spaniards, French, Czechs learn who defeated whom in which battle.
But if they don't have an enlightened cantor, they won't know what the wider context of the event was.
