No one will even tell you in schools that black people often sold into slavery by black people themselves, and that they were often the worst slavers.
No one will teach you at school that colonizers have often bought land from the Indians, they'll just tell you how brutally we've, the Europeans, slaughtered them, while killing each other long ago.
I've also spent some time in the U.S
I haven’t encountered anyone intentionally withholding the facts, but I’ve heard before that it’s happening and I think it’s a problem, I don’t deny that (for example, in Japan, WW2 atrocities are quite taboo).
My point was that history isn’t black and white, and that we tend to look at it from today’s point of view.
History doesn't care about someone's feelings, it's just what it's like and I think it's a fatal mistake to judge without looking at things from that time.
On the other hand, we should learn from it and never repeat this again.
By the way, while we're already with those Southern states, yes, the Confederate flag and the famous slavers are quite popular there, on the other hand, they also had some of their good achievements and I found it absurd to reject them.
In addition, the North was not much better, as a lot of people are idealizing it these days.
And a lot of people also forget that not everyone in the South were slavers, and a lot of things bothered.
I wouldn't compare this with the Russians, they deliberately omit some facts, they lie and manipulate, moreover, maybe our view doesn't exist with them (there was also a video from television on YT, where someone who started talking about our legionnaires and 1968).
What I found ridiculous in U.S. schools was the rise of Marxism and the idealization of communism, something their country has never experienced.
Overall, it seemed to me that in some universities it was appalling, those students were quite radicalized, and those schools supported them how many times.
And when I imagine that these people are going to be much older someday, I'm a little sick that this could be the voice of the majority, because it's among the young, and it's in the ruling elite.
It seems to me that, for example, feminism has long since achieved what it had and it is no longer about the same, it has become radicalized.
