TV has fallen the trend of Christmas movies, with two hundred premieres this year
Los Angeles – Cinemas, television stations and streaming platforms in the United States and other English-speaking countries have fallen through the trend of Christmas movies, and this year will present more than two hundred of them to their viewers.
This was calculated by IMDb's film database operator.
In recent years, the genre of Christmas family and romantic films has been scoring and significantly increasing the audience, which is why more and more of these films are being produced.
This year, four times more Christmas films have been made than in 2011 and twice as many as five years ago.
The IMDb database included in its total only those images that have the word Christmas in their title, so there will actually be a lot more festive films.
Movies that people traditionally associate with Christmas have always existed.
In the Czech Republic there are especially stories associated with this period, worldwide are also popular films Alone at Home, Love heavenly or the classic Christmas story Life is beautiful from 1946.
However, it was not until 2009, when the American cable station Hallmark came up with a special film series.
Her Advent project called Countdown to Christmas included four films and was very successful.
This station has already started to tweak its viewers for Christmas on October 22, presenting a total of 42 Christmas pictures.
Lifetime has 35 new Christmas-themed movies this year, and popular streaming platforms such as Netflix also contribute to the total.
“It doesn’t matter so much in this magical period, the important thing is that there are a lot of Christmas trees in the background and it’s snowing,” Brandon Gray, the author of I’ll Be Home for Christmas Movies, said.
“It’s a form of escape for the audience and a way to feel a little peace for at least two hours in the midst of all the festive madness and madness of the world we’ve been living in for the last few years.”
According to him, for example, Hallmark TV still uses the same recipe for its films, which is uniform but successful.
You have two people who fall in love, but then about half an hour before the end some misunderstanding arises, which, of course, is successfully solved and the two kiss.
It's like this over and over again, and as long as all the movies look similar and have a similar atmosphere, people are watching one after another, Gray adds.
