California will restrict the sale of weapons.
He wants to act like Texas in banning abortion.
California Governor Gavin Newsom on Saturday announced a plan to impose a ban on the sale and production of certain weapons in the most populous U.S. state, with the help of a legal mechanism used by Texas in its controversial anti-abortion law after detecting the embryo's heartbeat.
People would then be entitled to compensation in lawsuing anyone who manufactures or sells assault rifles and homemade firearms in California.
Newsom's announcement reacted to Friday's U.S. Supreme Courtion's opinion, which left the U.S. ban on abortion in force, even as it goes against a nearly 50-year-old precedent verdict that set the right for abortion across the U.S. to about 24 months pregnant.
However, the court did not now decide on the constitutionality of the whole law, but on the technical question arising from the innovative design of the measure.
Enforcement of the ban has been passed to the public in this case, making it impossible for Texas Republicans to challenge it by the usual judicial route.
"I am outraged by yesterday's (Friday's) U.S. Supreme Court decision, which authorized the maintenance of the Texas ban on the majority of abortion services and, to a large extent, supported the maneuver of Texas in order to protect its law," the California governor said in a statement.
“If states can now block a review of their laws by federal courts, then California will use that power to protect people’s lives.”
In cooperation with the state parliament and the Minister of Justice, he is said to have commissioned his subordinates to work on a measure that would entitle representatives of the public to enforce a ban on assault rifles and so-called ghost weapons.
In this way, home-made weapons that do not have serial numbers and that can be used to circumvent regulations are referred to.
Newsom wants "private citizens" to have the right to claim compensation of at least US$10 000 (over 220 000 Kč) and court costs from anyone who would produce, distribute or sell assault rifles, components to "ghost weapons" or sets for their production.
"If this is the most effective way to keep these horrific weapons off our streets, creating a threat of private lawsuits, then that's exactly what we should do," the California governor said.
For decades, California has banned the production and salute of military-style weapons, but in June, a federal judge blocked the ban as unconstitutional.
If the state were to actually renew the ban using a Texas template now, the words would be confirmed by the liberal member of the supreme court, Sonia Sotomayor, who, in a disapproving opinion on Friday's majority verdict, warned against extendinging the given legal mechanism to other U.S. states.
The Supreme Court, however, did not grant full immunity from the Texasian ban against judicial review and allowed abortion clinics to pursue lawsuits against selected officials of the southern U.S. state.
