Poland has threatened to halt payments to the EU budget
According to Ziobra, the European Commission would act in violation of the law if it took advantage of the new authority and, because of a dispute over the rule of law, halted the payment of money to Poland.
The commission has already postponed approval of Poland's plan to draw 36 billion euros from the EU's fund to rebuild economies affected by the covid-19 pandemic.
And it is under pressure from the European Parliament to move forward and apply a mechanism to remove EU subsidies to countries in breach of the principles of the rule of law.
"Poland should respond to this blackmail from the EU veto in all matters that require unanimousness," Ziobro, the head of Small Party Solidarity Poland, said, without whose votes the current government would lose a narrow majority in the Sejm.
"Poland should also consider its commitments in EU energy and climate policy that lead to a drastic rise in energy prices," Ziobro added.
If the dispute continues, I will demand that Poland halt its contributions to the EU.
This would be justifiable, given that the EU is unlawfully denying us funds from the common budget to which we are also contributing, the Polish minister added.
His party takes more radical stances in its approach to the EU than the ruling Law and Justice party.
According to the European Commission, the changes undergone by the Polish judiciary under Ziobrov’s tenure threaten its independence and subordinate it to politicians.
According to Ziobra, Brussels is building "impossible conditions because its goal is not the rule of law, but a change of government in Poland."
Warsaw is facing "political dictation carried out by blackmailing and trying to undermine the democratic decision of several million Poles," Ziobro said.
He stated that Poland should be a member of such an EU that is based on the partnership of sovereign states, and not on the government of the strongest and Brussels bureaucracy, which is not under democratic control.
He said his party would never accede to such concessions by Brussels, as a result of which Poland's sovereignty would be limited.
“We will never agree that Poland has the status of a colony,” he said.
