Defensing the system
When a prominent Czech lawyer and constitutional judge Vojtěch Cepl was answering a journalistic question in 1999 about what the Czech constitution meant to him – whether the sacred deed, to which he swears and which he learns at school or, on the contrary, an agreement that can be changed if necessary, he definitely leaned towards the first conception.
We once agreed on the democratic rules of our lives, which at the same time define who we are as the state and its citizens, and are better saved with changes.
And imagine: some nations even like their rules.
Just as Czechs like dumplings with pork and cabbage, Vojtěch Cepl asked the question at the time.
Recently, however, there has been an increasing opinion among lawyers that the Constitution of the Czech Republic needs changes.
For years it has been tested by situations that its creators (to which Vojtěch Cepl belonged) could not have predicted, for example, with the behavior of the directly elected president.
But Cepl was right about one thing.
Everything we know about such documents shows that political interference in their texts must become.
It is necessary to understand and actively defend the institution, only then it can be the key to managing most of the crises encountered by societies throughout history.
Among other things, the Constitution is an order of governance consisting of individual rules, which provide politicians for the limits of the game.
We are afraid that power will be misused against minorities or individuals, so we bind politicians with prohibitions.
At the same time, however, constitutional texts also allow politicians to have their power.
Covid doesn't choose, in Brno University Hospital they fight for the life of a several-month-old baby
Although coronavirus is known to be milder among children, there are also severe cases that hospitals have been dealing with especially recently.
“We know that children are at risk and affected less than adults, we are talking about 2 to 5 percent compared to adults,” Petr Dominik, head of the Department of Pediatric Anaesthesiology and Resuscitation at the Brno University Hospital and the Faculty of Medicine at Masaryk University, told the News.
The course tends to be much simpler, lighter, often without symptoms.
But there are child patients who are seriously ill with coronavirus, which we see especially in the last period," Dominik added.
According to the doctor, there are dozens of children who need slight supportive care.
It takes place at the clinic of childhood infectious diseases.
Very seriously ill children with coronavirus have only recently been at ARU.
Children with post-Covid syndromes were in the ward according to the doctor continuously throughout the year.
"There is now an increase in children with acute covid pneumonia, that is, with pneumonia, which requires staying on a resuscitation bed," he said, adding that the disease affects both adolescents and several-month-old babies alongside adults.
There are also children in hospitals in severe condition due to coronavirus.
“Currently, we have a child of several months and adolescent age,” he said.
However, he is pleased that there have been no children's deaths for coronavirus in the children's hospital.
According to the available data, six children between the ages of 0 and 14 died in the Czech Republic on December 6.
According to Dominik, in a children’s hospital – not only in the coronavirus ward – he is an integral part of the psychologist’s cooperation.
At the same time, it also draws attention to the fact that, as in adults, vaccination relieves the course of the disease and at the same time reduces the incidence of post-Covid syndrome.
“That is why we also recommend the application of the vaccination dose in children,” the doctor added.
