The legendary Nunes fell after seven years, Oliveira defended the belt
MMA experienced a great gala full of interesting results.
Things were happening at the UFC 269 tournament.
Outsider Julianna Peñová was able to beat legendary wrestler Amanda Nunes, who hasn't founded a slayer for seven years.
Charles Oliveira did not hesitate in the lightweights, showed a great strangulation against Dustin Poirier and defended the belt.
Kai Kara-France also scored, quickly swepting Cody Garbrandt with a technical KO in the first round.
Sean O'Malley also defeated his opponent.
Surprises that no one expected.
This brought the women's bantam weight struggle between famous Amanda Nunesova and Julianna Peñova.
The American entered the battle as an imaginary “dwarf”, because Nunes did not lose for seven years and grinded her teeth for another triumph.
Moreover, the beginning of the duel was in the spirit of paper assumptions.
Nunes started the way to victory very actively, and the opponent even gave a push kick to send her to the ground.
However, Peñová did not be forced to make any further mistake and tried unsuccessfully to attack herself with the help of a lever on her hand.
The second round was thrilling and very exciting for MMA fans.
Both opponents suffered a lot of excellent strokes and hard hooks.
In addition, Peňová got Nunes to the ground, where she started strangling her.
She had to give up the effort and tap the attack.
The American prepared a huge shock for everyone when she became the new champion.
The highlight of the gala was the battle between Charles Oliveira and Dustin Poirier for the lightweight title.
Initially, Poirier was doing better, but the card gradually began turning around.
In the latter, he tried to be more active Oliveira, who tried to beat his opponent with a lever on his hand.
Although he did not succeed much, then he created a lot of pressure, got his opponent on his back and filled him with a series of wounds.
Thanks to this, he won the second round.
In the third round, Oliveira showed a rear naked choke, to which Poirier resisted for a while, but subsequently he had to tap the strangulation.
The Brazilian thus defended the title, Poirier lost after two years.
In the next match, Sean O'Malley scored a sovereign triumph in the first round.
Subsequently, he conquered him with a series of precisely aimed strikes and recorded his 15th triumph.
Kara-France was then able to cope with Cody Garbrandt.
In the spring, Nunes celebrated another triumph with her young daughter, now after seven years she lost.
The hip, or the hip?
At first glance, it's nothing complicated.
Most nouns in Czech express only one grammatical genus, so it is not a problem to determine whether it is male, feminine or medium.
But then there is also a relatively large group of nouns, in which the genus is not settled.
Such nouns fluctuate between the two families.
When inclining, they acquire double endings and, in some cases, remain in an inclined form.
For example, the words “drooming” or “hips” are male and feminine, in the former they are inclined according to the “machine”, in the second according to “song”.
For the next group of nouns, there are already different shapes already in the first fall of the single number, for example: “row/row”, “koluben/kohlrabina” or “potato/potato” (in the sense of food).
Both shapes are standard, have the same meaning, and are therefore freely interchangeable.
Some expressions may vary regionally, such as “cucumber” in Bohemia and “cucumbers” in Moravia, but in this case the Moravian variant is non-standard, similarly there are other Czech-Moravian pairs of words: “ditch” and “ditch”, “doughnut” and “doughnut”, etc.
Some of the words, which penetrated into Czech from other languages, were originally non-inclined, but they gradually adopt Czech endings.
A typical example is the term “image”, which is both male and feminine, or the word “buffet”, which has remained inclined in the middle genus, but in the male has endings according to the “castle”.
