The Czech Republic is flooded with built-up houses, families do not have the money to finish
Construction material prices have risen more than 30 percent in recent weeks and months.
Many people put themselves in a difficult situation because of it.
It does not have the means to complete unfinished houses and banks refuse to increase their mortgage loans.
In addition to the prices of materials, the price of construction work is also increasing.
That's why people don't have enough money to finish their family houses.
In many cases, banks refuse to increase their mortgage loans, which creates extremely unpleasant situations.
At best, people are moving into unbuilt and unapproved houses.
At worst, uninhabitable houses are uninhabitable and families are forced to sell them because they cannot afford to repay their mortgages and still pay rent," says BHS economist Štěpán Křeček.
We'll build two or three family houses in a year, and that's what's happened to us at fifty percent.
It's difficult for us as a construction company in that we have to comply with some things contractually, even if the material has become more expensive.
So we do without earnings," said Zdeněk Slivoň, the owner of the construction company.
A lot of people are going to have financial problems.
If they were counting on the construction of the house to cost them five million, now it's going to cost them seven.
I think some of them are going to wait," Slivoň added.
Copper, irone, as well as plumbing and heating equipment were the most expensive.
However, construction companies are also battling labor shortages.
In the Czech Republic, graduates of building disciplines are lacking, and the influx of foreign workers is hampered by the pandemic.
At the moment, only the situation regarding the issuance of a building permit is more favorable.
“In October, building authorities issued 7 675 building permits, which is almost 10 percent more than a year ago,” Křeček said.
