How the pandemic has affected intimate life: A growing number of people under the age of 35 who are sex-free for a year
More and more young adults in the U.S. are living their lives without sex.
These are mainly religious people, according to the DailyMail website.
The survey showed that from 2008 to 2021, the proportion of people younger than 35 renouncing sex life has risen from eight to 21 percent.
Women between the ages of 18 and 35, who said they had no sexual intercourse in the last year, are more than ever.
Other factors also contribute to a decrease in the number of sexually active individuals, the Institute for Family Studies (IFS) said.
One of them may be the economic effects of the coronavirus pandemic and a higher unemployment percentage.
However, the presence of media, social networks and video games, which makes sex less and smaller a priority for young people, could also contribute.
"Since 2010, the proportion of men and women between the ages of 18 and 35 has been growing rapidly, stating that they have not had sex in the previous year," IFS researcher Lyman Stone said.
In people in marriages, sexual activity is more common, in 2021 only 5% of them said they have been without sex in the last year.
For unmarried people, it was 29%. Stone added that marriages below 35 are only a small percentage.
Fear of premarital intercourse and religious establishment also contributes to the decline of sexual activity.
Although spouses are more likely to be sexually active, the percentage of married and married under the age of 35 continues to decrease.
Young people share their opinion of premarital sex, about 30% think it’s the wrong thing, while about 70% think it’s okay.
“It’s true that among single individuals in this age group, they’re a minority, but their behavior shifts this trend,” Stone said.
For most of those who have a moral problem with premarital intercourse, the reason is religious.
Since 2008, for single people under the age of 35 who participate in religious gatherings more than once a month, the abstinence ratio has increased from 20 to almost 60%.
Among "fewer believers," the trend rose from 10% to 20%," Stone said.
Other factors also contribute to the decline in sexual activities, such as less social interaction and, above all, social drinking of alcohol during the pandemic.
The study also showed that the likelihood of sex is less in people without work or with lower incomes.
Another reason may be the expansion of digital media, which apparently reduces the need for sex.
People spend more time online, thereby “replacing” this need.
This trend has taken hold mainly during lockdown during the coronavirus pandemic.
