Germany's new chancellor, Olaf Scholz, after previous visits to Paris and Brussels on Sunday, flew to Warsaw, where Prime Minister Mateusz Morawiecki greeted him with military honours.
"We are opening a new chapter of mutual relations," Morawiecki said at a joint press conference after the meeting.
Scholz stressed that Europe must jointly signal that it will not accept violations of Ukraine's territorial integrity.
According to the chancellor, it is appropriate to solve the crisis, triggered by the disturbing movements of Russian troops near the Ukrainian border, using diplomatic negotiations, including within a “Normando group”, bringing together France, Germany, Russia and Ukraine.
Morawiecki said he informed the chancellor about the situation on the Polish border with Belarus, whose leader Alexander Lukashenko artificially provoked the migration crisis and uses people as living targets and a weapon, because night after night we record a hundred attempts to (illegally) cross the border.
He negotiated with the chancellor on further sanctions so that Lukashenko's regime and its patrons in the Kremlin will finally understand that we are determined to defend the EU's eastern border.
Scholz, according to the DPA news agency, assured that Warsaw enjoyed German support in the dispute with Belarus, and condemned Lukashenko's inhumane treatment of refugees.
