The Middle East has an unusually dry month.
Winter is the only time of the year when it rains.
"The almost complete absence of precipitation during November, as we've seen at some stations, is unusual," the Israeli Meteorological Service confirmed.
For example, Kfar Giladi villages in northern Israel report only six percent of the long-term rainfall average for November.
The two-day rain from this week was rather an exception.
It's good for us.
It hasn't rained long.
It's also the right Christmas backdrop, Nazareth resident Wasim Askar rejoiced.
Precipitation in Israel comes almost exclusively in the winter months, they are impactful and irregular.
Forests are dependent on winter rains.
Without them, they dry out and are prone to fires.
It's not just about forests, it's also about drinking water supplies and irrigation for farmers.
The largest freshwater source in Israel, Lake Galilee, filled to the brim this spring thanks to the three last rainy winters.
Since then, levels have dropped.
Water managers have warned of a drought a long time ago.
"It can be expected that, depending on global warming and climate changes, rainfall may decrease here," Uri Schor, a spokeswoman for Israel's Water Management Authority, predicted in 2018.
Israel can help itself with technologies such as desalination or wastewater recycling.
Weak countries such as Lebanon, Syria and Jordan are worse.
On the streets of Jordan's capital, Amman, the number of tank wagons is increasing.
Waters and private wells are drying up.
“I’ve had seventy to eighty percent this year, compared to the two previous years,” Imád Suleiman, the driver, reported in September.
Clashes between farmers and security forces erupted in Isfahan, Iran.
The reason for the protests was drought.
The riverbed of the local river was completely without water.
The region has had its driest November in many years.
Israel prepares military crackdown on Iran
Israel's defense minister said negotiations in Vienna yielded "no progress" and that it had informed Washington of preparations for an attack on Iranian nuclear facilities.
Minister Benny Gantz said on Saturday that he had ordered the Israeli military to prepare for the possibility of a military strike against Iran, reports Jonathan Lis.
Gantz, who resides in the U.S., is trying to persuade the Americans to escalate their pressure on Iran, but has also briefed Washington on military preparations.
During a press conference in Florida, Gantz said there was "no progress" in Vienna, and world powers "understand that the Iranians were messing with them."
