The next government quits, and the law on the involvement of municipalities in the selection of the repository nowhere
Minister Karel Havlíček’s proposal needs to be fundamentally revised
The government of Prime Minister Andrej Babiš is over, and the law, which was supposed to ensure respect for the interests of municipalities and their citizens in the selection and authorisation of a deep-sea repository of high radioactive waste, still does not exist.
The Legislative Council of the Government interrupted the discussion of a proposal submitted by the Ministry of Industry and Trade to the government after years of delays.
However, its content is in serious contradiction not only with the affected municipalities associated in the Platform against the deep repository, but also with the Union of Towns and Municipalities of the Czech Republic.
Self-governments from the law expect a greater reinforcement of their possibilities in deciding on the repository, which has been promised by mayors since 2011, when the first work on this legislation began and which is required by the Czech atomic law and the European directive.
We expect that the new government, in accordance with its coalition treaty, will revise the proposal in cooperation with municipalities.
The draft law from Minister Karel Havlíček, who has the Platform at its disposal, blames municipalities in particular: The proposed level of involvement of municipalities and the public in the process of deciding on the selection of a location for the repository is insufficient and cannot ensure respect for the interests of municipalities and their citizens.
It can be really effective only if municipalities or the public can influence whether the process continues in the locality at all.
This can be ensured by imposing the repository administration’s obligation to seek the consent of the municipalities concerned prior to the initiation of specific proceedings.
The presented proposal almost completely omits the involvement of the public and makes the citizens of municipalities practically mere statisticians during authorisation procedures.
The design lacks system compensation settings for municipalities for the entire process of searching and selecting a location for storage, permitting and operation.
Representatives of municipalities, according to today's legal norms, do not have many opportunities to defend the interests of their citizens in finding a place for storage.
Only in certain authorisation procedures may they submit their comments or appeal, but the Office or the Minister, in whose interest it is to issue a permit.
Any action does not have a suspensive effect on carrying out exploratory or construction work.
Co-decisioning the self-governments demanded by the Platform is a principle commonly used in many democratically advanced countries and certainly in those where they have already progressed in permitting the repository, such as in Sweden or Finland.
The preparation of the law is, among other things, another failure of the state administration, which hires external legal offices to prepare the legislation.
In this case, it is a contract with the law firm HAVEL & PARTNERS s.r.o. concluded by SÚRAO and the follow-up to contracts with attorney Jan Zemánek.
According to the register of contracts, the total amount for these works should amount to almost 4 million crowns.
Antonín Seknička, deputy mayor of the municipality of Cejle from Hrádek and a spokesperson of the Platform against Deep Repository, said: After the ministers of industry, who pushed the position of self-governments against state authorities in search of a deep repository of highly radioactive waste, we are expecting a more pronounced turnaround from the new government.
We also offer a helping hand.
We also thank the Union of Towns and Municipalities, which perceives the issue of insufficient rights of municipalities for such a fundamental construction similarly as directly concerned municipalities in selected localities.
The anti-deep repository platform brings together 51 members (35 municipalities and cities and 16 associations) in order to enforce a change in the state's approach to the management of spent nuclear fuel and other radioactive wastes, which will not be confined to a deep-sea repository.
In addition, the platform advocates that the decision on the selection of the site for possible imposition should be conditional on the prior consent of the municipalities concerned.
Actor John Goodman (69) was forced to lose weight by fear: He lost 90 kg.
Although he had no need to change anything about his lifestyle for many years, he was eventually frightened by doctors.
They told him that if he didn't lose weight, he would die.
And that worked.
Goodman gradually lost 90 kilograms, which is half his original weight of 180 kg, reports The Sun.
He boasted about his new character in Los Angeles at the premiere of the animated series The Freak Brothers.
He's a completely different person from the Roseanne sitcom!
John liked to joke that friends and family begged him to lose weight because his big body was causing furniture to crack.
"I've been shoving everything in my mouth," the actor said in an interview with AARP in 2018.
I wanted to do it slowly this time.
Moving, exercising.
I'm reaching an age where I can't afford to sit in place anymore, Goodman told ABC Goodman, whose transformation is amazing.
